JUCE patch: two process-wide flags that let an app's in-app test mode (--automation) and the test suite run without
touching the user's screen or window order.

ignoreWindowOrderForHitTests (macOS, Windows)
Why: JUCE decides which component a mouse event reaches with ComponentPeer::contains(), and on macOS and Windows that asks
the OS whether this window is the topmost one at the point. A window behind another window (or
another app) therefore never receives an event, even a synthetic one. The test mode drives its own windows by script and
must not depend on, nor change, the window order on the user's screen. The flag (default false) makes the macOS and
Windows peers' contains() skip their window-order check while it is true (NSViewComponentPeer asks the window server,
HWNDComponentPeer calls WindowFromPoint).

hideWindowsForAutomation (macOS only; Windows and Linux ignore it and compile unchanged)
Why: an automation run or a test run must not flash windows on the user's screen or take keyboard focus, so the user can
keep using the Mac. While the flag (default false) is true, every NSWindow the NSViewComponentPeer creates has alpha 0,
ignores mouse events (user clicks pass through; test-mode input goes straight to the ComponentPeer, not through NSEvents)
and has no shadow; setAlpha() keeps it at 0, so an app fade animation on a dialog cannot make it visible; and a real
input attempt on a blocked window no longer calls [NSApp activateIgnoringOtherApps:]. Because the app is then never active, the OS never reports a window as key: ComponentPeer::isFocused() follows the
JUCE-tracked focused peer instead (as for a plugin), toFront(true) gives the window JUCE focus directly, and an app-focus
change does not drop it. The foreground checks behind pop-up
menus, call-out boxes, tooltips, key commands and the active top-level window count a hidden process as foreground. The
windows are still ordered in and composited, so painting, display links and snapshots work as before. The flag is read when a window is created, so set it
before the first window.

With both flags false JUCE behaves exactly as stock.

Applied by cmake/ApplyJucePatch.cmake (FetchContent PATCH_COMMAND); mechanism and how to update it for a new
JUCE tag: docs/development/juce-patches.md. The JUCE files use CRLF line endings.

